{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TyiyVY3ixRYFwafBBESEKE","type":"tool_result","content":"**Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins (2000)**\n\n**Basics**\n- Released in North America on August 8, 2000; Europe on September 8, 2000; and Japan on November 30, 2000.\n- Platform: PlayStation.\n- Developer: Acquire. Publishers: Activision (worldwide) and Acquire (Japan).\n- Second entry in the Tenchu series and a prequel to *Tenchu: Stealth Assassins* (1998).\n- Director: Takuma Endo. Composer: Noriyuki Asakura.\n\n**Gameplay**\n- Third-person, 3D stealth gameplay. Players aim to stay undetected and either avoid or silently kill enemies.\n- Players use a variety of ninja tools, equipment, and stealth.\n- Missions take place in varied locations (farmland, bamboo forests, mountains, beaches, ships, villages, caverns) at different times of day.\n- New or changed features versus the first game, per development notes:\n  - Swimming controls were redesigned; the page says Acquire created five versions before Activision settled on one.\n  - More stealth kills were added.\n  - Enemy AI is more reactive, can hear the player's sounds better, and can call reinforcements.\n  - Escort missions were added, enabled by the civil-war setting.\n- The page does not describe body dragging/hiding, new moves, a ki/sense meter, or specific items.\n\n**Mission Structure and Ranking**\n- After each mission, the player receives a rank based on accomplishments such as enemies killed and times spotted.\n- High ranks unlock special gadgets for use in-game.\n- Missions emerge from the narrative and build on each other.\n- The page does not describe briefings, item selection, bosses, or rank names.\n\n**Mission Editor**\n- Widely praised feature that lets players create their own missions, including level layout, character placement, and objectives.\n- The Japanese version expanded the editor with new level-creation objects.\n\n**Playable Characters**\n- Rikimaru, Ayame, and Tatsumaru, three Azuma clan ninja.\n- The story is split into campaigns: first Rikimaru and Ayame, then Tatsumaru.\n\n**Setting and Premise**\n- Feudal Japan during the Sengoku period, four years before the first game.\n- After the death of Lord Godha, his uncle Motohide launches a coup against his son Matsunoshin.\n- The Azuma ninja support Matsunoshin, while Motohide is manipulated by a neighboring lord and the rogue Burning Dawn ninja group.\n\n**Tone**\n- The page doesn't explicitly describe the tone.\n- Development choices show a balance: the music was made more Japanese in tone, while Japanese elements were toned down for Western players.\n- The European release had less blood, no decapitations, and no shurikens due to UK laws.\n\n**Reception**\n- Metacritic: 77/100, with \"generally favorable\" reviews.\n- Next Generation gave it four out of five stars.\n- Sold over 101,000 units in Japan, according to Famitsu."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05c1fc6a-91e2-4dc5-aada-febbecf3984e","uuid":"67c32a75-5e13-4a3e-a94f-3c48f6cb1cb6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T00:58:24.962Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":262332,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"**Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins (2000)**\n\n**Basics**\n- Released in North America on August 8, 2000; Europe on September 8, 2000; and Japan on November 30, 2000.\n- Platform: PlayStation.\n- Developer: Acquire. Publishers: Activision (worldwide) and Acquire (Japan).\n- Second entry in the Tenchu series and a prequel to *Tenchu: Stealth Assassins* (1998).\n- Director: Takuma Endo. Composer: Noriyuki Asakura.\n\n**Gameplay**\n- Third-person, 3D stealth gameplay. Players aim to stay undetected and either avoid or silently kill enemies.\n- Players use a variety of ninja tools, equipment, and stealth.\n- Missions take place in varied locations (farmland, bamboo forests, mountains, beaches, ships, villages, caverns) at different times of day.\n- New or changed features versus the first game, per development notes:\n  - Swimming controls were redesigned; the page says Acquire created five versions before Activision settled on one.\n  - More stealth kills were added.\n  - Enemy AI is more reactive, can hear the player's sounds better, and can call reinforcements.\n  - Escort missions were added, enabled by the civil-war setting.\n- The page does not describe body dragging/hiding, new moves, a ki/sense meter, or specific items.\n\n**Mission Structure and Ranking**\n- After each mission, the player receives a rank based on accomplishments such as enemies killed and times spotted.\n- High ranks unlock special gadgets for use in-game.\n- Missions emerge from the narrative and build on each other.\n- The page does not describe briefings, item selection, bosses, or rank names.\n\n**Mission Editor**\n- Widely praised feature that lets players create their own missions, including level layout, character placement, and objectives.\n- The Japanese version expanded the editor with new level-creation objects.\n\n**Playable Characters**\n- Rikimaru, Ayame, and Tatsumaru, three Azuma clan ninja.\n- The story is split into campaigns: first Rikimaru and Ayame, then Tatsumaru.\n\n**Setting and Premise**\n- Feudal Japan during the Sengoku period, four years before the first game.\n- After the death of Lord Godha, his uncle Motohide launches a coup against his son Matsunoshin.\n- The Azuma ninja support Matsunoshin, while Motohide is manipulated by a neighboring lord and the rogue Burning Dawn ninja group.\n\n**Tone**\n- The page doesn't explicitly describe the tone.\n- Development choices show a balance: the music was made more Japanese in tone, while Japanese elements were toned down for Western players.\n- The European release had less blood, no decapitations, and no shurikens due to UK laws.\n\n**Reception**\n- Metacritic: 77/100, with \"generally favorable\" reviews.\n- Next Generation gave it four out of five stars.\n- Sold over 101,000 units in Japan, according to Famitsu.","durationMs":13868,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tenchu_2:_Birth_of_the_Stealth_Assassins"},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01TyiyVY3ixRYFwafBBESEKE","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
