{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012FVo6SMVvdAXLoJvCVZErD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins\n2\t\n3\t*An original isometric ninja stealth-action game, a playful riff on* Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins.\n4\t*Build plan for the developers (human or AI) who will build it from TASK.md and this file alone.*\n5\t\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t## 0. At a glance\n9\t\n10\t**Pitch.** Kasugai Province, the Sengoku era. The granaries are full and the people starve. In the shadow of Kokaku\n11\tCastle, a humble shop, the **Jubako-ya**, sells two-tier lunchboxes (jubako) to the lord's household. Its boxes have two\n12\ttiers. So does the shop: one tier for the rice and one for the blade. You play one of its two apprentices, **Sojiro**\n13\t(katana, steady) or **Kaede** (twin short blades, quick), through the night they pass their trial and become true\n14\t**Lunchbox Assassins**. The story is played straight, in the source's earnest, terse feudal-drama voice. The jokes are\n15\tdeadpan and come from the world (poisoned rice balls, rank names taken from bento grades), never from winking.\n16\t\n17\t**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** take a mission at the shop, pack your bento of ninja\n18\ttools, slip into a lantern-lit night location unseen, silence guards with Silent Cuts, reach the target, survive the\n19\tboss, escape, and earn a rank that unlocks new tools.\n20\t\n21\t**Genre:** third-person ninja stealth-action, with light melee combat and boss duels.\n22\t**Perspective:** isometric third person on the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, pitch 30), rotatable in 45-degree steps and\n23\tzoomable, with `threequarter` as a Settings alternative (\"Camera angle: Low / High\"). Tenchu's behind-the-back 3D camera\n24\tis not available in this 2D engine. Its job (limited sight, the ki sense, looking around corners) is rebuilt as\n25\t**Shadow Sight** (section 5.5): enemies the ninja can't see are hidden unless the Ki gauge senses them.\n26\t\n27\t**Scope of the demo (all required, no stubs):**\n28\t\n29\t- A training mission and three story missions, each with a boss. That is about 45 minutes of play.\n30\t- Two playable characters.\n31\t- 10 tools, packed in the bento.\n32\t- 5 guard types plus civilians and disguised enemies.\n33\t- Silent Cuts from 5 directions.\n34\t- Grappling hook, swimming and diving, body dragging, wall hugging, light and shadow, noise, alarm bells with\n35\t  reinforcements, and an escort mission.\n36\t- Ranks and unlocks, and saves.\n37\t- Story cutscenes with gibberish voices.\n38\t- Dev mode with live balance sliders.\n39\t- An attract mode.\n40\t- A Mission Editor (the \"Workshop\", after Tenchu 2's Mission Editor).\n41\t- AI Hub ghost-mode multiplayer for 1–4 players, plus its standalone twin, the Shadow Trial, where you race your own\n42\t  best ghost.\n43\t- An About/Credits screen.\n44\t\n45\t**Deliverables (names follow TASK.md's bracket pattern with GameTitle = `Bentou2`, SOURCE = `Tenchu2`):**\n46\t\n47\t- Dev folder: `Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734/` (every dev file goes in here).\n48\t- Single-file game in the root: `Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734.html`.\n49\t- `exit-interview.json` in the root, beside the HTML.\n50\t\n51\tIf your own TASK.md carries a different model or timestamp suffix, use yours. The title stays.\n52\t\n53\t---\n54\t\n55\t## 1. Title selection (done; do not change)\n56\t\n57\tTASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, chosen by a real RNG from 50 silently brainstormed candidates.\n58\tThe 50 candidates were written into a script and one was drawn with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)` (the OS CSPRNG).\n59\tThe draw was 26, so entry #27 won: **\"Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins\"**. It riffs on *Tenchu 2: Birth of\n60\tthe Stealth Assassins* (Tenchu → Bentou/bento; Stealth → Lunchbox). The world and story build on it: the \"2\" is the\n61\ttwo-tier box and the two apprentices. The title screen shows **BENTOU 2** in large type with the subtitle *Birth of the\n62\tLunchbox Assassins*.\n63\t\n64\t---\n65\t\n66\t## 2. What makes the source distinctive, and how this game captures it\n67\t\n68\tFacts about the source come from research (Wikipedia's article on the game; see the Source Information in section 10).\n69\tTenchu 2 (Acquire / Activision, PlayStation, 2000) is a prequel to the first Tenchu. Three Azuma clan ninja (two\n70\tcampaigns) support a young lord against a coup in Sengoku Japan. Compared with the first game, it adds:\n71\t\n72\t- redesigned swimming;\n73\t- more stealth kills;\n74\t- guards that hear better and call reinforcements;\n75\t- escort missions;\n76\t- ranks per mission that unlock gadgets;\n77\t- a widely praised Mission Editor.\n78\t\n79\tIts signatures, and our *original* version of each (no names, characters, levels, music or art are taken from it):\n80\t\n81\t| Source signature | Our version |\n82\t|---|---|\n83\t| Stealth first; detection is dangerous; the camera limits what you see | **Shadow Sight** hides enemies the ninja cannot see. **Light and shadow, noise and cover** decide detection. Being seen brings shouts, the alarm bell and reinforcements |\n84\t| Ki meter: a number showing how near enemies are, colored by their awareness | **Ki Gauge** (bottom left): 0–99 for the nearest sensed enemy; teal, yellow, orange or red with `?` or `!`; a taiko heartbeat when high. **Ki Focus** (hold L2/Tab) extends the sense |\n85\t| Instant cinematic stealth kills with an on-screen kanji splash | **Silent Cuts**: 5 directions (back, side, unaware front, from above, from the water), different for each character, with slow motion, a zoom and a red brush splash of **静斬** (\"silent cut\", an original choice; not the source's kanji) |\n86\t| Grappling hook to rooftops | **Kaginawa**: the only way onto roofs. Auto-target, an aim mode, and mouse targeting |\n87\t| Ninja tools carried into missions; items chosen before each mission | **The Bento**: 8 compartments (2 tiers × 4) packed on a \"Pack the Bento\" screen. Food tools (rice balls, poisoned rice, sleep dango, sour plum) and steel tools (shuriken, smoke, caltrops, firecracker, fire pot, straw cloak) |\n88\t| Poisoned rice that guards eat | The **Poison Rice Ball**, the game's signature lunchbox item; hungry guards complain about garrison rice |\n89\t| Ranks after each mission from kills and times spotted; high ranks unlock gadgets | Results scroll with a hanko rank stamp: **Grand Kaiseki / Makunouchi / Ekiben / Hinomaru / Leftovers** (bento grades). High ranks unlock tools |\n90\t| Lord's briefing, mission, boss at the end | Briefings in the shop or the lord's garden; every mission ends in a boss duel |\n91\t| Tenchu 2: swimming; enemies call reinforcements; escort missions | Moats and lakes to swim and dive (unseen, with a breath limit); Undertow kills; an alarm bell; the escort of the courier Tobei |\n92\t| Tenchu 2: Mission Editor | **Workshop**: a full controller-friendly editor on the same level format the campaign uses; save, test, play, export JSON |\n93\t| Several playable ninja, one story | Sojiro and Kaede. Same missions, different stats, moves, Silent Cuts and lines |\n94\t| Tone: earnest, melodramatic feudal drama, stoic ninja, a traitor, a hint of the supernatural, comic guard chatter | Same register: a starving province, a corrupt magistrate, a traitor chamberlain in a cursed oni mask, a rival ninja band, guards who mutter about bad rice |\n95\t\n96\t---\n97\t\n98\t## 3. Technology and architecture\n99\t\n100\t### 3.1 Stack and folder layout\n101\t\n102\t- **Engine:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`), the full\n103\t  readable engine `my-3d2dge.js` (one file, no dependencies). Read its `API.md` whole, `kits/stealth.js` whole (it\n104\t  already has guards with `E.sees` / `E.Suspicion`, FlowField chasing, takedowns, parry, Shell HUD, layered music) and\n105\t  `docs/ASSETS.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. The game starts from the STEALTH kit's slice, reorganized into\n106\t  the modules below (copy its patterns; delete the kit's starter shell, title menu and other slices).\n107\t- **Multiplayer:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `session.js` 1.4.0, pasted unmodified\n108\t  (`raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`). The harness (`harness.html`, `bridge.js`,\n109\t  `round.js`) is for tests only.\n110\t- **Tooling:** Node 20+ (the machine has Node 24), Playwright with Chromium (the handoff's `package.json` pins it) for\n111\t  the browser checks, and Python's `http.server` only for the hub harness.\n112\t- **No other libraries, no external fonts, no image or audio files.** Everything is drawn and synthesized in code.\n113\t\n114\t```text\n115\t<root>/                                        (the folder holding TASK.md and this PLAN.md)\n116\t  Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734.html   final single file (built)\n117\t  exit-interview.json\n118\t  Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734/       dev folder\n119\t    engine/my-3d2dge.js        copy of the handoff engine, patched (section 3.3); engine/PATCHES.md lists every change\n120\t    vendor/aihub/session.js    unmodified client; harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only)\n121\t    handoff/                   API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md, kits/stealth.js, tools/*.mjs (check,\n122\t                               filmstrip, replay, asset-sheet, asset-find), package.json: reference + tools\n123\t    src/                       the game, in load order (section 3.4)\n124\t    index.html                 dev page: loads engine + each src file with <script> tags (works from file://)\n125\t    tools/build.mjs            bundles into the single HTML in <root>\n126\t    tests/run.mjs              THE test command (section 9) + tests/*.test.mjs + tests/browser/*.mjs\n127\t    shots/                     screenshots and filmstrips (output)\n128\t```\n129\t\n130\tEnvironment rules (from TASK.md):\n131\t\n132\t- Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need; other agents share this machine.\n133\t- Prefer `file://` and headless tools.\n134\t- The hub harness needs a server. Start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the folder being served, parse\n135\t  the port it prints, and confirm the served page is your game before trusting a result.\n136\t- Stop only the processes you started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n137\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small, nearly full tmpfs. Keep temp files inside the dev folder.\n138\t\n139\t### 3.2 Build and bundle (`tools/build.mjs`)\n140\t\n141\tOne command, `node tools/build.mjs`, writes `<root>/Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734.html`:\n142\t\n143\t```html\n144\t<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n145\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n146\t<script>/* session.js, byte-for-byte unmodified (starts with \"/*! aihub-session 1.4.0\") */</script>\n147\t<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n148\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n149\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n150\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n151\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n152\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Silent kills, unseen, fast: top ninja score wins\">\n153\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move WASD/stick · Strike click/□ · Jump Space/✕ · Crouch C/○ · Tool R/R2\">\n154\t<title>Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins</title>\n155\t<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}</style>\n156\t</head><body>\n157\t<!-- E.bootSplash('BENTOU 2') markup -->\n158\t<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n159\t<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (patched) */</script>\n160\t<script>/* the game: src/*.js concatenated, in order, inside ONE IIFE: (() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })(); */</script>\n161\t</body></html>\n162\t```\n163\t\n164\t- Escape every `</script` inside the inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n165\t- The session client must be the **first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`**, unmodified.\n166\t- The goal meta is at most 60 characters and the howto meta at most 80.\n167\t- `tools/build.mjs --js` writes only the concatenated game script to `dev/build/game.js`. The tests load that, so tests\n168\t  and the bundle run the same code.\n169\t- The build fails loudly if a `src` file is missing from the order list or if `TODO|FIXME|placeholder|lorem` appears in\n170\t  `src`.\n171\t\n172\t### 3.3 Engine patches (do them in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, mark each `// BENTOU PATCH n`, list them in `PATCHES.md` and on the About screen)\n173\t\n174\t1. **Pixel font glyphs.** Add `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5×7 font `G57` (7-row bitmaps), so `input.label()` text (which returns\n175\t   them for PlayStation pads) renders in canvas text and dialogs instead of `?`.\n176\t2. **Button symbols setting.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs`, make the default `'playstation'` and the options\n177\t   `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`. TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with an option for Xbox A B X Y.\n178\t3. **Humanoid gear** (in the head and weapon draw code, around the face block that draws eyes and mouth):\n179\t   - `mask: { color, style: 'cloth' | 'oni' }`. Cloth covers nose and mouth and keeps the eyes; it skips the mouth and\n180\t     also draws in portraits. Oni draws a red face with tusks, heavy brows and glowing eyes.\n181\t   - New hats: `hat: 'jingasa'` (wide shallow cone) and `hat: 'kabuto'` (helmet with a crest; `crest` color).\n182\t   - New weapons:\n183\t     - `weapon: 'spear'` (yari: a staff with a blade tip);\n184\t     - `weapon: 'bow'` (a curved stave with a string to the draw hand; drawn while `point` is true);\n185\t     - `weapon: 'club'` (kanabo: thick, tapered, studded).\n186\t   - `offhand: { len }`: a second short blade in the left hand (twin kodachi, twin kama).\n187\t   - `sashimono: color`: a small back banner on a pole (samurai).\n188\t\n189\t   Check each with `node handoff/tools/filmstrip.mjs` and the Animation Lab views.\n190\t4. **Shell: custom rows.** In `Shell._render`, add `if (s.rows) for (const k of v(s.rows)) add(this._row(k));`, so a\n191\t   custom screen can show settings rows (sliders, toggles, choices). In `_settingsRows`, skip the group `'Dev'`, so dev\n192\t   sliders never appear in the player's Settings.\n193\t5. **Shell: hover moves focus.** Add a `pointermove` listener on the screen that focuses the focusable under the\n194\t   pointer (`preventScroll: true`). TASK.md requires that hover moves the focus.\n195\t6. **Shell: 10-foot scale.** Add a CSS variable `--ui` (default\n196\t   `clamp(1, (innerHeight/720) * userScale, 3)`) and apply it with `zoom: var(--ui)` on `.esh-mod`, `.esh-ttl>div`,\n197\t   `.esh-toast`, `.esh-pr`, `.esh-tut`. Recompute it on resize. `userScale` comes from the setting \"Interface size\"\n198\t   (Normal 1, Large 1.25, Huge 1.5). Raise the base font to 16 px and buttons to a minimum height of 52 px.\n199\t7. **Music track envelopes.** In `_note`, honor per-track `pluck: true` (non-held exponential decay over\n200\t   `ring`, default 1.1 s), `attack` (default .006) and `release`. Koto, shamisen and harp plucks then ring and decay.\n201\t   Shakuhachi uses a slow attack (.09) with `vib`.\n202\t8. **Voice bus.** Expose `audio.voiceBus` (a gain on `_master`, created in `_init`), and apply the game setting\n203\t   `voices` (0–1) to it. The voice synth (section 5.17) connects there.\n204\t9. **Hub pause guard.** While `window.AIHub && AIHub.active` and `Pad8` (Create) is held, ignore the `pause` action\n205\t   (Options). The hub owns Create + Options.\n206\t\n207\tKeep every patch small, and don't refactor the engine.\n208\t\n209\t### 3.4 Module map (`src/`, concatenated in this order inside one IIFE)\n210\t\n211\t| File | Responsibility | Key exports (on the shared `B` namespace object) |\n212\t|---|---|---|\n213\t| `00-core.js` | namespace `B`, registries `B.def(kind, id, spec)` / `B.get(kind, id)` / `B.all(kind)`, event bus `B.on/off/emit`, `rand()` (gameplay, Math.random) vs `crand` (cosmetic E.rng stream), math helpers | `B`, `def`, `bus` |\n214\t| `01-tune.js` | every balance number as a `game.settings` entry in group `'Dev'` (section 5.20 table), `B.T(key)` getter with difficulty multipliers | `TUNE`, `T()` |\n215\t| `02-input.js` | action map (section 4.1), wheel listener, `B.intentFromInput()` (stick + keys, d-pad excluded in play), device and touch-only detection, \"any input\" clock for attract | `PlayerInput` |\n216\t| `03-glyphs.js` | pixel button icons per device and family; `B.glyph(g, action, x, y, o)`, and inline `{a:jump}` tokens in canvas text | `Glyph` |\n217\t| `04-camera.js` | follow, 45-degree rotation, zoom, look-ahead (mouse lean, Focus + right stick), cinematic overrides (Silent Cut zoom, boss intro, cutscenes) | `Cam` |\n218\t| `10-audio.js` | `audio.define` sfx set, songs with layers (section 5.17), `B.music.mood(level)` | `Sfx`, `Songs`, `Music` |\n219\t| `11-voice.js` | gibberish formant voice synth, speaker profiles, dialog hook, barks with distance and panning | `Voice` |\n220\t| `20-assets.js` | `E.SHAPES` / `E.MATERIALS` / `E.PATTERNS` for Japanese props, floor textures, wall `decorate` functions, the kawara roof-cap renderer, string sprites (item icons, crest, kanji, moon, eyes) | `Art` |\n221\t| `21-rigs.js` | character factories (section 6.1), `Hound` quadruped class, joint post-processing helpers (arms-back ninja run, wall-hug pose, eating, sleeping, mouth cover) | `Rigs`, `Hound` |\n222\t| `30-world.js` | level JSON → `World` (TileMap + navigation map + light map + hide map + water + anchors + props + lights + zones); queries (`lightAt`, `isHide`, `isDeep`, `anchorsNear`, `nearestBell`, `zoneAt`) | `World` |\n223\t| `31-actors.js` | base `Actor` (a Mover plus rig plus HP plus status); spawn helpers | `Actor` |\n224\t| `40-hero.js` | hero controller from an *intent* (section 5.1): traversal, swim, hug, drag, focus, noise emission, tool use, combat hooks | `Hero` |\n225\t| `41-silentcut.js` | Silent Cut detection and `E.ACTIONS` entries per variant and character; cinematic and splash | `SilentCut` |\n226\t| `42-grapple.js` | anchor search, targeting UI, `E.ACTIONS.grapple`, rope drawing | `Grapple` |\n227\t| `43-combat.js` | damage pipeline (`B.damage(src, dst, amount, kind)`), hero combos and jump attack, block, parry, riposte, dodge, i-frames, hit fx | `Combat` |\n228\t| `44-tools.js` | the 10 tools: data and behavior (projectiles, lobs, clouds, traps, baits), `Bento` loadout model | `Tools`, `Bento` |\n229\t| `50-perception.js` | sight (exposure from light, crouch, cover, motion, look-up limit, smoke), hearing (`B.noise(x,y,z,loud,kind)`), smell, body discovery, Shadow Sight visibility, Ki values | `Perception` |\n230\t| `51-guard-brain.js` | guard state machine on top of `E.Suspicion` (section 5.4), patrol, post, sleep, eat, chat, search, chase, fight, flee, bark scheduling, alarm and bell logic | `Brain` |\n231\t| `52-enemies.js` | archetype registry: ashigaru, samurai, archer, ninja, hound, civilians, disguised | `def('enemy',...)` |\n232\t| `53-bosses.js` | `Boss` base with phases and pattern scripts (`game.script` generators); Ittetsu, Gonroku, Hotaru, Kurogane | `def('boss',...)` |\n233\t| `54-escort.js` | the escort follower (Tobei) | `Escort` |\n234\t| `60-mission.js` | mission runtime: objectives, triggers, timers, checkpoints, fail states, pickups, interactions | `Mission` |\n235\t| `61-score.js` | scoring events → score, ranks, unlocks, best records, results data | `Score` |\n236\t| `62-story.js` | all text: crawl, briefings, cutscene scripts, boss lines, barks, results comments (section 6.6) | `Story` |\n237\t| `63-cutscene.js` | cutscene player (staged rigs, camera tweens, letterbox, `E.Dialog` with portraits and voices, skip) | `Cutscene` |\n238\t| `64-levels-*.js` | one file per level: training, m1, m2, m3, delivery (data only, `def('level', id, {...})`) | level data |\n239\t| `65-missions.js` | mission metadata (`def('mission', id, {...})`): title, level, briefing, loadout hint, par, unlock rules, next | mission data |\n240\t| `70-hud.js` | canvas HUD (section 4.4): health, Ki gauge, tool box, prompts, tutorial cards, toasts, boss bar, timer, breath, stamina, awareness marks, threat arrows, letterbox, splash | `Hud` |\n241\t| `71-scenes-menu.js` | boot, title (idle timer), main menu, character select, mission select, about/credits, touch-only notice | scenes |\n242\t| `72-scenes-story.js` | briefing, bento packing, results, game over, ending and credits | scenes |\n243\t| `73-scene-mission.js` | the gameplay scene used by story, trial, hub, attract and workshop test play | `MissionScene` |\n244\t| `74-dev.js` | dev mode screens, cheats, overlays, sliders (section 5.20) | `Dev` |\n245\t| `75-workshop.js` | the Mission Editor (section 5.21) | scene `workshop` |\n246\t| `80-autopilot.js` | intent-producing autopilot (beats), shared by attract mode and the playthrough test; per-mission routes | `Autopilot` |\n247\t| `81-attract.js` | attract director and vignettes (section 5.19) | scene `attract` |\n248\t| `82-hub.js` | AI Hub ghost mode, own card, ghost send and draw, progress and result, end handling; Shadow Trial ghost recording | `Hub`, scene `hubCard` |\n249\t| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game(...)`, scene registration, boot steps, saves wiring, `game.debug`, `window.__BENTOU` | — |\n250\t\n251\tArchitecture rules (the Emberdeep lessons from AI_GUIDE):\n252\t\n253\t- **A spine, then content.** Content (levels, enemies, tools, bosses, lines) is data in registries and never edits the\n254\t  core. Systems talk through the **event bus**:\n255\t\n256\t  ```text\n257\t  noise, seen, detected, alert, bodyFound, silentCut, kill, damage, heroDeath, toolUsed,\n258\t  objective, checkpoint, bell, boss, missionEnd\n259\t  ```\n260\t\n261\t  Score, HUD toasts, music mood, barks, hub progress and tests all listen. None of them is called directly from the\n262\t  combat or AI code.\n263\t- **One intent interface for the hero.** The player's input adapter, the autopilot and the tests all produce the same\n264\t  intent object (section 5.1), so attract mode never touches `game.input` and can't be mistaken for player input.\n265\t- **Game logic in world units, with `dt` everywhere.** Gameplay randomness uses `rand()` in update only; cosmetic\n266\t  randomness in draw uses `crand` (an `E.rng` stream). Draw code never consumes `Math.random`, which keeps hub-seeded\n267\t  runs and tests deterministic at any refresh rate.\n268\t- **No DOM at load in headless.** Guard every `matchMedia`, `document` and `navigator` use, so the game runs under\n269\t  `node:vm` (as `game/test.mjs` does).\n270\t\n271\t### 3.5 Scenes and flow\n272\t\n273\t```text\n274\tboot ─┬─(AIHub.active)──► hubCard ──start──► mission(mode:'hub') ──end──► hub results overlay\n275\t      └─► title ◄──────── any input ──── attract (after 10 s idle on title or main menu)\n276\t           │ menu: Continue · New Story · Missions · Shadow Trial · Workshop · Settings · About\n277\t           ├─ New Story ─► charSelect ─► story(crawl+prologue) ─► briefing ─► bento ─► mission ─► results ─► story ─► …\n278\t           │                                                     (training → m1 → m2 → m3 → ending+credits → title)\n279\t           ├─ Missions ─► missionSelect (character + mission, best ranks) ─► briefing ─► bento ─► mission ─► results\n280\t           ├─ Shadow Trial ─► bento(trial rules) ─► mission(mode:'trial', with your best ghost) ─► results(trial board)\n281\t           ├─ Workshop ─► workshop ◄─► mission(mode:'workshop')\n282\t           └─ About ─► about (pages)\n283\tmission: pausable. Pause = Shell pause: Resume · Restart from checkpoint · Restart mission · Objectives · Dev Mode ·\n284\t         Settings · Controls · Full screen · Quit to title   (hub: Resume · Settings · Controls only)\n285\tmission death ─► \"Your box is empty\" overlay: Retry from checkpoint · Restart mission · Quit\n286\t```\n287\t\n288\tScene options:\n289\t\n290\t- `mission`:\n291\t\n292\t  ```js\n293\t  { pausable: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'], res: 'ps1', expand: true, input: B.INPUT,\n294\t    touch: [], camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1.15, tall: 1.35, rotate: true, wheel: false } }\n295\t  ```\n296\t\n297\t- Menu scenes use the same input preset and `touch: []`.\n298\t- The title is a side-view diorama (`view: 'side'`) with a `PlatformMap` ledge and an `E.Backdrop`.\n299\t\n300\t### 3.6 Data formats (the campaign and the Workshop share them exactly)"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"05c1fc6a-91e2-4dc5-aada-febbecf3984e","uuid":"e482f5d3-3565-4329-8542-0614388debe6","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:30:10.937Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174734__xb43d63/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins\n\n*An original isometric ninja stealth-action game, a playful riff on* Tenchu 2: Birth of the Stealth Assassins.\n*Build plan for the developers (human or AI) who will build it from TASK.md and this file alone.*\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n**Pitch.** Kasugai Province, the Sengoku era. The granaries are full and the people starve. In the shadow of Kokaku\nCastle, a humble shop, the **Jubako-ya**, sells two-tier lunchboxes (jubako) to the lord's household. Its boxes have two\ntiers. So does the shop: one tier for the rice and one for the blade. You play one of its two apprentices, **Sojiro**\n(katana, steady) or **Kaede** (twin short blades, quick), through the night they pass their trial and become true\n**Lunchbox Assassins**. The story is played straight, in the source's earnest, terse feudal-drama voice. The jokes are\ndeadpan and come from the world (poisoned rice balls, rank names taken from bento grades), never from winking.\n\n**Core loop (one sentence, put it at the top of the game code):** take a mission at the shop, pack your bento of ninja\ntools, slip into a lantern-lit night location unseen, silence guards with Silent Cuts, reach the target, survive the\nboss, escape, and earn a rank that unlocks new tools.\n\n**Genre:** third-person ninja stealth-action, with light melee combat and boss duels.\n**Perspective:** isometric third person on the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, pitch 30), rotatable in 45-degree steps and\nzoomable, with `threequarter` as a Settings alternative (\"Camera angle: Low / High\"). Tenchu's behind-the-back 3D camera\nis not available in this 2D engine. Its job (limited sight, the ki sense, looking around corners) is rebuilt as\n**Shadow Sight** (section 5.5): enemies the ninja can't see are hidden unless the Ki gauge senses them.\n\n**Scope of the demo (all required, no stubs):**\n\n- A training mission and three story missions, each with a boss. That is about 45 minutes of play.\n- Two playable characters.\n- 10 tools, packed in the bento.\n- 5 guard types plus civilians and disguised enemies.\n- Silent Cuts from 5 directions.\n- Grappling hook, swimming and diving, body dragging, wall hugging, light and shadow, noise, alarm bells with\n  reinforcements, and an escort mission.\n- Ranks and unlocks, and saves.\n- Story cutscenes with gibberish voices.\n- Dev mode with live balance sliders.\n- An attract mode.\n- A Mission Editor (the \"Workshop\", after Tenchu 2's Mission Editor).\n- AI Hub ghost-mode multiplayer for 1–4 players, plus its standalone twin, the Shadow Trial, where you race your own\n  best ghost.\n- An About/Credits screen.\n\n**Deliverables (names follow TASK.md's bracket pattern with GameTitle = `Bentou2`, SOURCE = `Tenchu2`):**\n\n- Dev folder: `Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734/` (every dev file goes in here).\n- Single-file game in the root: `Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734.html`.\n- `exit-interview.json` in the root, beside the HTML.\n\nIf your own TASK.md carries a different model or timestamp suffix, use yours. The title stays.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title selection (done; do not change)\n\nTASK.md asks for a playful riff on the source's name, chosen by a real RNG from 50 silently brainstormed candidates.\nThe 50 candidates were written into a script and one was drawn with Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)` (the OS CSPRNG).\nThe draw was 26, so entry #27 won: **\"Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins\"**. It riffs on *Tenchu 2: Birth of\nthe Stealth Assassins* (Tenchu → Bentou/bento; Stealth → Lunchbox). The world and story build on it: the \"2\" is the\ntwo-tier box and the two apprentices. The title screen shows **BENTOU 2** in large type with the subtitle *Birth of the\nLunchbox Assassins*.\n\n---\n\n## 2. What makes the source distinctive, and how this game captures it\n\nFacts about the source come from research (Wikipedia's article on the game; see the Source Information in section 10).\nTenchu 2 (Acquire / Activision, PlayStation, 2000) is a prequel to the first Tenchu. Three Azuma clan ninja (two\ncampaigns) support a young lord against a coup in Sengoku Japan. Compared with the first game, it adds:\n\n- redesigned swimming;\n- more stealth kills;\n- guards that hear better and call reinforcements;\n- escort missions;\n- ranks per mission that unlock gadgets;\n- a widely praised Mission Editor.\n\nIts signatures, and our *original* version of each (no names, characters, levels, music or art are taken from it):\n\n| Source signature | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| Stealth first; detection is dangerous; the camera limits what you see | **Shadow Sight** hides enemies the ninja cannot see. **Light and shadow, noise and cover** decide detection. Being seen brings shouts, the alarm bell and reinforcements |\n| Ki meter: a number showing how near enemies are, colored by their awareness | **Ki Gauge** (bottom left): 0–99 for the nearest sensed enemy; teal, yellow, orange or red with `?` or `!`; a taiko heartbeat when high. **Ki Focus** (hold L2/Tab) extends the sense |\n| Instant cinematic stealth kills with an on-screen kanji splash | **Silent Cuts**: 5 directions (back, side, unaware front, from above, from the water), different for each character, with slow motion, a zoom and a red brush splash of **静斬** (\"silent cut\", an original choice; not the source's kanji) |\n| Grappling hook to rooftops | **Kaginawa**: the only way onto roofs. Auto-target, an aim mode, and mouse targeting |\n| Ninja tools carried into missions; items chosen before each mission | **The Bento**: 8 compartments (2 tiers × 4) packed on a \"Pack the Bento\" screen. Food tools (rice balls, poisoned rice, sleep dango, sour plum) and steel tools (shuriken, smoke, caltrops, firecracker, fire pot, straw cloak) |\n| Poisoned rice that guards eat | The **Poison Rice Ball**, the game's signature lunchbox item; hungry guards complain about garrison rice |\n| Ranks after each mission from kills and times spotted; high ranks unlock gadgets | Results scroll with a hanko rank stamp: **Grand Kaiseki / Makunouchi / Ekiben / Hinomaru / Leftovers** (bento grades). High ranks unlock tools |\n| Lord's briefing, mission, boss at the end | Briefings in the shop or the lord's garden; every mission ends in a boss duel |\n| Tenchu 2: swimming; enemies call reinforcements; escort missions | Moats and lakes to swim and dive (unseen, with a breath limit); Undertow kills; an alarm bell; the escort of the courier Tobei |\n| Tenchu 2: Mission Editor | **Workshop**: a full controller-friendly editor on the same level format the campaign uses; save, test, play, export JSON |\n| Several playable ninja, one story | Sojiro and Kaede. Same missions, different stats, moves, Silent Cuts and lines |\n| Tone: earnest, melodramatic feudal drama, stoic ninja, a traitor, a hint of the supernatural, comic guard chatter | Same register: a starving province, a corrupt magistrate, a traitor chamberlain in a cursed oni mask, a rival ninja band, guards who mutter about bad rice |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Technology and architecture\n\n### 3.1 Stack and folder layout\n\n- **Engine:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`), the full\n  readable engine `my-3d2dge.js` (one file, no dependencies). Read its `API.md` whole, `kits/stealth.js` whole (it\n  already has guards with `E.sees` / `E.Suspicion`, FlowField chasing, takedowns, parry, Shell HUD, layered music) and\n  `docs/ASSETS.md` whole. Grep `API-INDEX.md` for names. The game starts from the STEALTH kit's slice, reorganized into\n  the modules below (copy its patterns; delete the kit's starter shell, title menu and other slices).\n- **Multiplayer:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client `session.js` 1.4.0, pasted unmodified\n  (`raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`). The harness (`harness.html`, `bridge.js`,\n  `round.js`) is for tests only.\n- **Tooling:** Node 20+ (the machine has Node 24), Playwright with Chromium (the handoff's `package.json` pins it) for\n  the browser checks, and Python's `http.server` only for the hub harness.\n- **No other libraries, no external fonts, no image or audio files.** Everything is drawn and synthesized in code.\n\n```text\n<root>/                                        (the folder holding TASK.md and this PLAN.md)\n  Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734.html   final single file (built)\n  exit-interview.json\n  Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734/       dev folder\n    engine/my-3d2dge.js        copy of the handoff engine, patched (section 3.3); engine/PATCHES.md lists every change\n    vendor/aihub/session.js    unmodified client; harness.html, bridge.js, round.js (tests only)\n    handoff/                   API.md, API-INDEX.md, AI_GUIDE.md, docs/ASSETS.md, kits/stealth.js, tools/*.mjs (check,\n                               filmstrip, replay, asset-sheet, asset-find), package.json: reference + tools\n    src/                       the game, in load order (section 3.4)\n    index.html                 dev page: loads engine + each src file with <script> tags (works from file://)\n    tools/build.mjs            bundles into the single HTML in <root>\n    tests/run.mjs              THE test command (section 9) + tests/*.test.mjs + tests/browser/*.mjs\n    shots/                     screenshots and filmstrips (output)\n```\n\nEnvironment rules (from TASK.md):\n\n- Read nothing outside your folder that you don't need; other agents share this machine.\n- Prefer `file://` and headless tools.\n- The hub harness needs a server. Start `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` from the folder being served, parse\n  the port it prints, and confirm the served page is your game before trusting a result.\n- Stop only the processes you started, by PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall` or `fuser -k`.\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small, nearly full tmpfs. Keep temp files inside the dev folder.\n\n### 3.2 Build and bundle (`tools/build.mjs`)\n\nOne command, `node tools/build.mjs`, writes `<root>/Bentou2-Tenchu2-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174734.html`:\n\n```html\n<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head>\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>/* session.js, byte-for-byte unmodified (starts with \"/*! aihub-session 1.4.0\") */</script>\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"5\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Silent kills, unseen, fast: top ninja score wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Move WASD/stick · Strike click/□ · Jump Space/✕ · Crouch C/○ · Tool R/R2\">\n<title>Bentou 2: Birth of the Lunchbox Assassins</title>\n<style>html,body{margin:0;height:100%;background:#000;overflow:hidden}#screen{width:100%;height:100%;display:block;image-rendering:pixelated;touch-action:none}</style>\n</head><body>\n<!-- E.bootSplash('BENTOU 2') markup -->\n<canvas id=\"screen\"></canvas>\n<script>/* engine/my-3d2dge.js (patched) */</script>\n<script>/* the game: src/*.js concatenated, in order, inside ONE IIFE: (() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })(); */</script>\n</body></html>\n```\n\n- Escape every `</script` inside the inlined code as `<\\/script`.\n- The session client must be the **first script in `<head>`, right after `<meta charset>`**, unmodified.\n- The goal meta is at most 60 characters and the howto meta at most 80.\n- `tools/build.mjs --js` writes only the concatenated game script to `dev/build/game.js`. The tests load that, so tests\n  and the bundle run the same code.\n- The build fails loudly if a `src` file is missing from the order list or if `TODO|FIXME|placeholder|lorem` appears in\n  `src`.\n\n### 3.3 Engine patches (do them in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, mark each `// BENTOU PATCH n`, list them in `PATCHES.md` and on the About screen)\n\n1. **Pixel font glyphs.** Add `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5×7 font `G57` (7-row bitmaps), so `input.label()` text (which returns\n   them for PlayStation pads) renders in canvas text and dialogs instead of `?`.\n2. **Button symbols setting.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs`, make the default `'playstation'` and the options\n   `[['playstation','PlayStation'],['xbox','Xbox']]`. TASK.md: PlayStation glyphs, with an option for Xbox A B X Y.\n3. **Humanoid gear** (in the head and weapon draw code, around the face block that draws eyes and mouth):\n   - `mask: { color, style: 'cloth' | 'oni' }`. Cloth covers nose and mouth and keeps the eyes; it skips the mouth and\n     also draws in portraits. Oni draws a red face with tusks, heavy brows and glowing eyes.\n   - New hats: `hat: 'jingasa'` (wide shallow cone) and `hat: 'kabuto'` (helmet with a crest; `crest` color).\n   - New weapons:\n     - `weapon: 'spear'` (yari: a staff with a blade tip);\n     - `weapon: 'bow'` (a curved stave with a string to the draw hand; drawn while `point` is true);\n     - `weapon: 'club'` (kanabo: thick, tapered, studded).\n   - `offhand: { len }`: a second short blade in the left hand (twin kodachi, twin kama).\n   - `sashimono: color`: a small back banner on a pole (samurai).\n\n   Check each with `node handoff/tools/filmstrip.mjs` and the Animation Lab views.\n4. **Shell: custom rows.** In `Shell._render`, add `if (s.rows) for (const k of v(s.rows)) add(this._row(k));`, so a\n   custom screen can show settings rows (sliders, toggles, choices). In `_settingsRows`, skip the group `'Dev'`, so dev\n   sliders never appear in the player's Settings.\n5. **Shell: hover moves focus.** Add a `pointermove` listener on the screen that focuses the focusable under the\n   pointer (`preventScroll: true`). TASK.md requires that hover moves the focus.\n6. **Shell: 10-foot scale.** Add a CSS variable `--ui` (default\n   `clamp(1, (innerHeight/720) * userScale, 3)`) and apply it with `zoom: var(--ui)` on `.esh-mod`, `.esh-ttl>div`,\n   `.esh-toast`, `.esh-pr`, `.esh-tut`. Recompute it on resize. `userScale` comes from the setting \"Interface size\"\n   (Normal 1, Large 1.25, Huge 1.5). Raise the base font to 16 px and buttons to a minimum height of 52 px.\n7. **Music track envelopes.** In `_note`, honor per-track `pluck: true` (non-held exponential decay over\n   `ring`, default 1.1 s), `attack` (default .006) and `release`. Koto, shamisen and harp plucks then ring and decay.\n   Shakuhachi uses a slow attack (.09) with `vib`.\n8. **Voice bus.** Expose `audio.voiceBus` (a gain on `_master`, created in `_init`), and apply the game setting\n   `voices` (0–1) to it. The voice synth (section 5.17) connects there.\n9. **Hub pause guard.** While `window.AIHub && AIHub.active` and `Pad8` (Create) is held, ignore the `pause` action\n   (Options). The hub owns Create + Options.\n\nKeep every patch small, and don't refactor the engine.\n\n### 3.4 Module map (`src/`, concatenated in this order inside one IIFE)\n\n| File | Responsibility | Key exports (on the shared `B` namespace object) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | namespace `B`, registries `B.def(kind, id, spec)` / `B.get(kind, id)` / `B.all(kind)`, event bus `B.on/off/emit`, `rand()` (gameplay, Math.random) vs `crand` (cosmetic E.rng stream), math helpers | `B`, `def`, `bus` |\n| `01-tune.js` | every balance number as a `game.settings` entry in group `'Dev'` (section 5.20 table), `B.T(key)` getter with difficulty multipliers | `TUNE`, `T()` |\n| `02-input.js` | action map (section 4.1), wheel listener, `B.intentFromInput()` (stick + keys, d-pad excluded in play), device and touch-only detection, \"any input\" clock for attract | `PlayerInput` |\n| `03-glyphs.js` | pixel button icons per device and family; `B.glyph(g, action, x, y, o)`, and inline `{a:jump}` tokens in canvas text | `Glyph` |\n| `04-camera.js` | follow, 45-degree rotation, zoom, look-ahead (mouse lean, Focus + right stick), cinematic overrides (Silent Cut zoom, boss intro, cutscenes) | `Cam` |\n| `10-audio.js` | `audio.define` sfx set, songs with layers (section 5.17), `B.music.mood(level)` | `Sfx`, `Songs`, `Music` |\n| `11-voice.js` | gibberish formant voice synth, speaker profiles, dialog hook, barks with distance and panning | `Voice` |\n| `20-assets.js` | `E.SHAPES` / `E.MATERIALS` / `E.PATTERNS` for Japanese props, floor textures, wall `decorate` functions, the kawara roof-cap renderer, string sprites (item icons, crest, kanji, moon, eyes) | `Art` |\n| `21-rigs.js` | character factories (section 6.1), `Hound` quadruped class, joint post-processing helpers (arms-back ninja run, wall-hug pose, eating, sleeping, mouth cover) | `Rigs`, `Hound` |\n| `30-world.js` | level JSON → `World` (TileMap + navigation map + light map + hide map + water + anchors + props + lights + zones); queries (`lightAt`, `isHide`, `isDeep`, `anchorsNear`, `nearestBell`, `zoneAt`) | `World` |\n| `31-actors.js` | base `Actor` (a Mover plus rig plus HP plus status); spawn helpers | `Actor` |\n| `40-hero.js` | hero controller from an *intent* (section 5.1): traversal, swim, hug, drag, focus, noise emission, tool use, combat hooks | `Hero` |\n| `41-silentcut.js` | Silent Cut detection and `E.ACTIONS` entries per variant and character; cinematic and splash | `SilentCut` |\n| `42-grapple.js` | anchor search, targeting UI, `E.ACTIONS.grapple`, rope drawing | `Grapple` |\n| `43-combat.js` | damage pipeline (`B.damage(src, dst, amount, kind)`), hero combos and jump attack, block, parry, riposte, dodge, i-frames, hit fx | `Combat` |\n| `44-tools.js` | the 10 tools: data and behavior (projectiles, lobs, clouds, traps, baits), `Bento` loadout model | `Tools`, `Bento` |\n| `50-perception.js` | sight (exposure from light, crouch, cover, motion, look-up limit, smoke), hearing (`B.noise(x,y,z,loud,kind)`), smell, body discovery, Shadow Sight visibility, Ki values | `Perception` |\n| `51-guard-brain.js` | guard state machine on top of `E.Suspicion` (section 5.4), patrol, post, sleep, eat, chat, search, chase, fight, flee, bark scheduling, alarm and bell logic | `Brain` |\n| `52-enemies.js` | archetype registry: ashigaru, samurai, archer, ninja, hound, civilians, disguised | `def('enemy',...)` |\n| `53-bosses.js` | `Boss` base with phases and pattern scripts (`game.script` generators); Ittetsu, Gonroku, Hotaru, Kurogane | `def('boss',...)` |\n| `54-escort.js` | the escort follower (Tobei) | `Escort` |\n| `60-mission.js` | mission runtime: objectives, triggers, timers, checkpoints, fail states, pickups, interactions | `Mission` |\n| `61-score.js` | scoring events → score, ranks, unlocks, best records, results data | `Score` |\n| `62-story.js` | all text: crawl, briefings, cutscene scripts, boss lines, barks, results comments (section 6.6) | `Story` |\n| `63-cutscene.js` | cutscene player (staged rigs, camera tweens, letterbox, `E.Dialog` with portraits and voices, skip) | `Cutscene` |\n| `64-levels-*.js` | one file per level: training, m1, m2, m3, delivery (data only, `def('level', id, {...})`) | level data |\n| `65-missions.js` | mission metadata (`def('mission', id, {...})`): title, level, briefing, loadout hint, par, unlock rules, next | mission data |\n| `70-hud.js` | canvas HUD (section 4.4): health, Ki gauge, tool box, prompts, tutorial cards, toasts, boss bar, timer, breath, stamina, awareness marks, threat arrows, letterbox, splash | `Hud` |\n| `71-scenes-menu.js` | boot, title (idle timer), main menu, character select, mission select, about/credits, touch-only notice | scenes |\n| `72-scenes-story.js` | briefing, bento packing, results, game over, ending and credits | scenes |\n| `73-scene-mission.js` | the gameplay scene used by story, trial, hub, attract and workshop test play | `MissionScene` |\n| `74-dev.js` | dev mode screens, cheats, overlays, sliders (section 5.20) | `Dev` |\n| `75-workshop.js` | the Mission Editor (section 5.21) | scene `workshop` |\n| `80-autopilot.js` | intent-producing autopilot (beats), shared by attract mode and the playthrough test; per-mission routes | `Autopilot` |\n| `81-attract.js` | attract director and vignettes (section 5.19) | scene `attract` |\n| `82-hub.js` | AI Hub ghost mode, own card, ghost send and draw, progress and result, end handling; Shadow Trial ghost recording | `Hub`, scene `hubCard` |\n| `90-main.js` | `new E.Game(...)`, scene registration, boot steps, saves wiring, `game.debug`, `window.__BENTOU` | — |\n\nArchitecture rules (the Emberdeep lessons from AI_GUIDE):\n\n- **A spine, then content.** Content (levels, enemies, tools, bosses, lines) is data in registries and never edits the\n  core. Systems talk through the **event bus**:\n\n  ```text\n  noise, seen, detected, alert, bodyFound, silentCut, kill, damage, heroDeath, toolUsed,\n  objective, checkpoint, bell, boss, missionEnd\n  ```\n\n  Score, HUD toasts, music mood, barks, hub progress and tests all listen. None of them is called directly from the\n  combat or AI code.\n- **One intent interface for the hero.** The player's input adapter, the autopilot and the tests all produce the same\n  intent object (section 5.1), so attract mode never touches `game.input` and can't be mistaken for player input.\n- **Game logic in world units, with `dt` everywhere.** Gameplay randomness uses `rand()` in update only; cosmetic\n  randomness in draw uses `crand` (an `E.rng` stream). Draw code never consumes `Math.random`, which keeps hub-seeded\n  runs and tests deterministic at any refresh rate.\n- **No DOM at load in headless.** Guard every `matchMedia`, `document` and `navigator` use, so the game runs under\n  `node:vm` (as `game/test.mjs` does).\n\n### 3.5 Scenes and flow\n\n```text\nboot ─┬─(AIHub.active)──► hubCard ──start──► mission(mode:'hub') ──end──► hub results overlay\n      └─► title ◄──────── any input ──── attract (after 10 s idle on title or main menu)\n           │ menu: Continue · New Story · Missions · Shadow Trial · Workshop · Settings · About\n           ├─ New Story ─► charSelect ─► story(crawl+prologue) ─► briefing ─► bento ─► mission ─► results ─► story ─► …\n           │                                                     (training → m1 → m2 → m3 → ending+credits → title)\n           ├─ Missions ─► missionSelect (character + mission, best ranks) ─► briefing ─► bento ─► mission ─► results\n           ├─ Shadow Trial ─► bento(trial rules) ─► mission(mode:'trial', with your best ghost) ─► results(trial board)\n           ├─ Workshop ─► workshop ◄─► mission(mode:'workshop')\n           └─ About ─► about (pages)\nmission: pausable. Pause = Shell pause: Resume · Restart from checkpoint · Restart mission · Objectives · Dev Mode ·\n         Settings · Controls · Full screen · Quit to title   (hub: Resume · Settings · Controls only)\nmission death ─► \"Your box is empty\" overlay: Retry from checkpoint · Restart mission · Quit\n```\n\nScene options:\n\n- `mission`:\n\n  ```js\n  { pausable: true, view: 'iso', views: ['iso', 'threequarter'], res: 'ps1', expand: true, input: B.INPUT,\n    touch: [], camera: { zoom: [.75, 2], start: 1.15, tall: 1.35, rotate: true, wheel: false } }\n  ```\n\n- Menu scenes use the same input preset and `touch: []`.\n- The title is a side-view diorama (`view: 'side'`) with a `PlatformMap` ledge and an `E.Backdrop`.\n\n### 3.6 Data formats (the campaign and the Workshop share them exactly)","numLines":300,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1883}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_012FVo6SMVvdAXLoJvCVZErD","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
